Do not chase a tail taller than the window, and let the whole subagent bar go up

Two things, one of them a lurch I introduced.

The growth correction is "how far the tail is cut off by", which stops being a small
number the moment the tail row is taller than the window: it becomes the height of the
whole message. The recorder caught it on the phone — corrections of 3625px, each one
replacing every row on screen, the old oversize teleport back in a new form. The rule
that prevented it was in the code this replaced and I dropped it: a message taller
than the screen is not chased. The reader is left where they are, the answer carries on
below the fold, and they read it at their own pace. A test grows the tail to twice the
viewport and asserts the list does not move.

And the subagent bar's way up is now the whole seat rather than the words in it. The
tap was on the title row, so it was as wide as the arrow and the name and one line
tall; a tap a few pixels right of the title, in space belonging to nothing else, did
nothing. The seat fills the bar's height and its full width.

332 green.